SRR8097475	61.27 S 43.92 W	Southern Ocean: Southeast Orkney Island	-	01-Jan-2015	2018-10-23T00:00:00.000	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	Malaspina viral metaG Antarct_38	Identifying viral communities during a global deep-ocean expedition. Malaspina viral metaG Antarct_38 from Orkney Island (southeast).	SRP166536	PRJNA365146	6625657	SRA797829	SRS3969577	10280304	SAMN10280304	408172	marine metagenome	-	seawater	-	-	-	Illumina HiSeq 2500	2521121790	4778656230	AXWTY	WGS	METAGENOMIC	other	PAIRED	Metagenome	Environment	Genome	Whole	marine metagenome...:Marine viral communities from the Global Malaspina Expedition - Malaspina viral metaG Antarct_38 metagenome	Identifying viral communities during a global deep-ocean expedition
DRR232766	39.019030 N 141.72820 E	Japan	10 m	2015-03	2020-06-04T00:00:00.000	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	Illumina MiSeq paired end sequencing of SAMD00231496	Keywords: GSC:MIxS;MIMS:5.0	DRP004618	PRJDB5708	11014238	DRA005744	DRS140145	15102321	SAMD00231496	408172	marine metagenome	coastral sea water	-	aquatic biome	sea water	-	Illumina MiSeq	433112156	711818103	-	WGS	GENOMIC	RANDOM	PAIRED NOMINAL_LENGTH="1000"	Metagenome	Environment	Genome	Whole	marine metagenome...:Metagenomic sequences of the Ofunato Bay 2015	The Ofunato Bay in Iwate Prefecture, Japan, is a deep coastal bay located at the center of the Sanriku Rias coast and considered an economically and environmentally important asset. Here we present the first whole genome sequencing (WGS) study on the microbial communities of the Bay where surface water samples were collected in 2015 from three stations along its length to cover the entire bay. We sequenced 0.2-??m filter fraction, targeting the free-living fraction only, among the sequentially size-fractionated samples of 20.0-, 5.0-, 0.8- and 0.2-??m filters,. As expected, Proteobacteria (Alpha, Beta and Gamma) were the most abundant phyla in the Ofunato Bay followed by Bacteroidetes, Firmicutes, Actinobacteria, Cyanobacteria and Tenericutes. We detected a wide range of heterotrophic bacteria, Archaea and eukaryotic algae. The majority of these bacteria are considered significant in oceans and marine ecology.
