SRR7597481	34.119200 S 151.226700 E	Australia:Port Hacking NRS	0.0	2017-03-21	2018-04-19T00:00:00.000	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	Marine_metagenomics	-	SRP114822	PRJNA385736	6048400	SRA744809	SRS3194229	8953136	SAMN08953136	408172	marine metagenome	-	Pelagic_Public	-	-	-	Illumina HiSeq 2500	4172703367	6741362500	102.100.100/35478	WGS	METAGENOMIC	PCR	PAIRED	Metagenome	Environment	Genome	Whole	marine metagenome...:Marine metagenomes Metagenome	Marine amplicons from Australian marine (benthic and pelagic environments
SRR4342136	28.5833 N 91.25 W	USA:Gulf of Mexico,E4	28.3	2013-07-24	2016-09-21T00:00:00.000	-	-	-	-	35.81	-	-	-	25.08	-	-	Metagenomics of GOM dead zone: site E4	Keywords: GSC:MIxS;MIMS:5.0	SRP090800	PRJNA340003	3244683	SRA481748	SRS1726552	5791320	SAMN05791320	408172	marine metagenome	water	marine	marine pelagic	continental shelf	niskin bottle	Illumina HiSeq 2000	3516531298	5489779856	Site-E4-DNA	WGS	METAGENOMIC	size fractionation	PAIRED	Raw sequence reads	Environment	Genome	Whole	marine metagenome...:Metagenomic reconstruction of bacterioplankton community metabolism in the northern Gulf of Mexico Dead Zone	Raw reads of bacterioplankton metagenomes and metatranscriptomes from the northern Gulf of Mexico dead zone.Marine regions that experience low dissolved oxygen (DO) from seasonal to long-term time scales, a.k.a. dead zones, are increasing in number and severity around the globe with deleterious effects on ecology and economics. One of the largest of these occurs on the continental shelf of the northern Gulf of Mexico (nGOM) as a result of eutrophication-enhanced bacterioplankton respiration and strong stratification. The effects of this perturbation on microbial assemblages, and therefore the underlying potential for biogeochemical cycling, have only begun to be explored. Here we present 77 high-quality genomes (33 of which are &gt;70% complete) reconstructed from whole community metagenomic data spanning six sites of varying DO concentrations during the 2013 nGOM dead zone. All but two genomes could be classified into 17 named bacterial and archaeal phyla, and many represented the most abundant organisms observed previously in the dead zone (e.g., Thaumarchaeota, Marine group II Euryarchaeota, Synechococcus, Actinobacteria, SAR406). Surprisingly, we recovered near complete genomes from the Candidate Phyla Parcubacteria (OD1), Peregrinibacteria, Latescibacteria (WS3), and ACD39. Metabolic reconstruction predicted roles for many organisms in various phases of nitrogen, carbon, and sulfur cycling. Importantly, many of the most abundant and/or most active taxa were predicted aerobes, suggesting electron donor-based niche partitioning. This work provides an important biogeochemical map for multiple phyla, helping to resolve the impacts of hypoxia on nutrient flow in the dead zone.
