SRR2163470	34.2415 N 120.1155 W	USA: Santa Barbara Basin	-	Nov-2012	2016-03-19T06:44:36.530	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	Anoxic sulfidic sediment incubation	-	SRP056300	PRJNA278788	1362929	SRA247243	SRS876363	3431442	SAMN03431442	412755	marine sediment metagenome	-	marine sediment	-	-	-	Illumina HiSeq 2000	6205983429	9896062012	Post-incubation metatranscriptome (HiSeq)	RNA-Seq	METATRANSCRIPTOMIC	RANDOM	PAIRED NOMINAL_SDEV="0.0E0"	Transcriptome or Gene expression	Environment	Transcriptome	Whole	marine sediment metagenome...:Marine sediment incubations Transcriptome or Gene expression	Microorganisms can impact phosphorus (P) cycling in aquatic sediments by accumulating and hydrolyzing intracellular polyphosphate (poly-P). In particular, poly-P utilization by large sulfur bacteria has been implicated in phosphate mineral formation in hypoxic marine zones, and might constitute an important sink in the global P cycle. Here, we combined incubation experiments with metatranscriptomics to compare microbial poly-P utilization in sediments exposed to different redox conditions. We incubated sediments from two locations: methane-seep sediments collected near Barbados that had a white sulfur-oxidizing biofilm community at the sediment-water interface, and sulfidic Santa Barbara Basin (SBB) sediments with no visible sulfur-oxidizing mat.
SRR1718852	22.808683 N 158.0503 W	Pacific Ocean:North Pacific Subtropical Gyre	150 m	2012-08-26	2015-02-24T06:57:36.120	-	-	-	-	35.242	aerobic	-	-	-80C	-	-	HOE-DYLAN 9 seawater metagenome	HOE-DYLAN 9 seawater from 150 m at station ALOHA, particulates collected on 0.2 micron filters	SRP051172	PRJNA270248	1163432	SRA216343	SRS794578	3264853	SAMN03264853	412755	marine sediment metagenome	ocean water	-	marine pelagic biome	marine oligotrophic desert	Niskin, CTD	Illumina MiSeq	447942732	738586831	HD9_SW_150	WGS	METAGENOMIC	size fractionation	PAIRED NOMINAL_SDEV="0.0E0"	Genome sequencing and assembly	Environment	Genome	Whole	marine sediment metagenome...:Marine sediment trap metagenomes	This study explores the taxonomic and metabolic diversity of microbes associated with sinking particles in the North Pacific Subtropical Gyre. Marine particles are hotspots of microbial activity, yet the microbial taxa and biochemical processes involved in carbon and energy cycling on sinking particles are poorly understood. Metagenomes generated in this study revealed new insights into the important taxa and metabolisms involved in microbial processing of marine particles.
