SRR1627907	8.59234666667 N 84.077358333 W	Costa Rica	93.98	2011-04-03	2014-10-23T11:20:50.780	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	Costa Rica margin subsurface Metagenome- 1378_B11H3_93p98mbsf- 1	Keywords: GSC:MIxS;MIMS:5.0	SRP049239	PRJNA264715	1071584	SRA194126	SRS728416	3140187	SAMN03140187	412755	marine sediment metagenome	sediment	-	ocean sediment	sediment	-	Illumina HiSeq 1000	2242417208	3697927128	Costa Rica margin subsurface Metagenome- 1378_B11H3_93p98mbsf- 1	WGS	METAGENOMIC	RANDOM	PAIRED	Metagenome	Environment	Genome	Whole	marine sediment metagenome...:Costa Rica margin subsurface Metagenome	The subsurface environment off the shore of Costa Rica has been targeted by the broader scientific community for intense research efforts due to the unique history and current conditions of the subduction system. The unusualness of the system revolves around the hypothesized changes in its behavior from those of an accretionary subduction system to those of an erosive subduction system. Here, we selected 3 of these samples from one site within this subsurface location for Illumina shotgun metagenomics. These data allow not only an independent view of taxonomy and phylogenetic relationships, but also provide insight on the functional properties of the communities from 3 different depths within this unique subduction zone system.
SRR1627906	8.59234666667 N 84.077358333 W	Costa Rica	32.39	2011-04-03	2014-10-23T11:20:50.630	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	Costa Rica margin subsurface Metagenome- 1378_B04H6_32p39mbsf- 1	Keywords: GSC:MIxS;MIMS:5.0	SRP049239	PRJNA264715	1071579	SRA194126	SRS728415	3140186	SAMN03140186	412755	marine sediment metagenome	sediment	-	ocean sediment	sediment	-	Illumina HiSeq 1000	2269354244	3830639040	Costa Rica margin subsurface Metagenome- 1378_B04H6_32p39mbsf- 1	WGS	METAGENOMIC	RANDOM	PAIRED	Metagenome	Environment	Genome	Whole	marine sediment metagenome...:Costa Rica margin subsurface Metagenome	The subsurface environment off the shore of Costa Rica has been targeted by the broader scientific community for intense research efforts due to the unique history and current conditions of the subduction system. The unusualness of the system revolves around the hypothesized changes in its behavior from those of an accretionary subduction system to those of an erosive subduction system. Here, we selected 3 of these samples from one site within this subsurface location for Illumina shotgun metagenomics. These data allow not only an independent view of taxonomy and phylogenetic relationships, but also provide insight on the functional properties of the communities from 3 different depths within this unique subduction zone system.
SRR1627905	8.59234666667 N 84.077358333 W	Costa Rica	2.88	2011-04-02	2014-10-23T11:20:50.257	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	Costa Rica margin subsurface Metagenome- 1378_B01H2_2p88mbsf- 1	Keywords: GSC:MIxS;MIMS:5.0	SRP049239	PRJNA264715	1071568	SRA194126	SRS728414	3140185	SAMN03140185	412755	marine sediment metagenome	sediment	-	ocean sediment	sediment	-	Illumina HiSeq 1000	5705597664	9359164656	Costa Rica margin subsurface Metagenome- 1378_B01H2_2p88mbsf- 1	WGS	METAGENOMIC	RANDOM	PAIRED	Metagenome	Environment	Genome	Whole	marine sediment metagenome...:Costa Rica margin subsurface Metagenome	The subsurface environment off the shore of Costa Rica has been targeted by the broader scientific community for intense research efforts due to the unique history and current conditions of the subduction system. The unusualness of the system revolves around the hypothesized changes in its behavior from those of an accretionary subduction system to those of an erosive subduction system. Here, we selected 3 of these samples from one site within this subsurface location for Illumina shotgun metagenomics. These data allow not only an independent view of taxonomy and phylogenetic relationships, but also provide insight on the functional properties of the communities from 3 different depths within this unique subduction zone system.
SRR1964817	11.2811 N 93.1 E	Andaman Sea	554	25-Jul-2006	2013-08-29T00:00:00.000	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-80	-	-	shotgun metagenomic sequencing Andaman Sea Site 17A 554 mbsf	Keywords: GSC:MIxS;MIMARKS:5.0	SRP029382	PRJNA217520	1434478	SRA253110	SRS475104	2339375	SAMN02339375	412755	marine sediment metagenome	sediment	-	marine	sediment	-	Illumina HiSeq 1000	1555605573	2530469592	Site17A_554mbsf_metagenomic	WGS	METAGENOMIC	RANDOM	PAIRED	Targeted Locus (Loci)	Environment	Genome	Targeted Locus/Loci	marine sediment metagenome...:Subseafloor sediments hosting gas hydrates Targeted Locus (Loci)	Sediment samples were obtained from the Andaman Sea at Site 17A during the National Gas Hydrates Program (NGHP) Expedition 01 in 2006 (Scientists &apos;06) and microbiology described in (Briggs et al. &apos;12). This site exists in a back arc basin that contains mainly marine deposited nanofossil ooze that was deposited at a rate of 5.6 cm/kyr. The uniquely low geothermal gradient (2.1° C/100 m) allows gas hydrate to exist deep into the sediment column (300 to 600 meters below seafloor or mbsf) making the gas hydrate deposits found at this location among the deepest discovered to date. Geologic horizons demarcate this gas hydrate occurrence zone (GHOZ) as evidenced by increasing porosity from 50% to 63% at 300 mbsf and a seismically observed bottom-simulating reflector where the methane is believed to exist as a free gas at 600 meters below seafloor (mbsf). Biogenic methane is present above 600 mbsf and transitioning to mixed thermogenic/ biogenic methane. In addition, the Andaman Sea sediments are unique because the total organic carbon (TOC) is less than 1% by weight, while other similar continental margins where microbiology has been examined contain 1-11% TOC (Meister et al. &apos;05).
